const VALUE_CHAIN_EXTENSIONS: LearningLesson[] = [
  lesson("From buried organics to a petroleum system", "The PEA posters trace petroleum from organic-rich sediment through burial, heating and generation, then place that process within a working petroleum system. Source rock, maturation, migration, reservoir, seal, trap and timing must align for an accumulation to form and remain preserved.", "Working system = source + maturation + migration + reservoir + seal + trap + timing", "A prospect can have a reservoir and closure but still fail if charge or seal is absent. Geoscientists combine regional basin history and seismic or well evidence to test the full system.", "A prospect is a connected geological hypothesis, not just a mapped closure.", "Which petroleum-system element provides the storage space for movable hydrocarbons?", ["Reservoir rock", "Source rock", "Regional seal"], 0, "Reservoir pore space stores fluids; source rock generates hydrocarbons and a seal limits their escape."),
  lesson("Read basins, traps and seals", "Sedimentary basins preserve layered deposits and the burial history that can mature organic material. Structural or stratigraphic changes can form traps, but a working trap also needs a seal and correct timing. Trap shape alone does not demonstrate that hydrocarbons are present.", "Trap effectiveness depends on closure + seal + charge + timing", "A fault can create a trap or leak through one, depending on its rocks and history. Treat the map interpretation as a hypothesis to test with wells and seismic data.", "Geometry, sealing capacity and charge must be evaluated together.", "What does a seal do in a petroleum trap?", ["Restricts fluids from escaping the reservoir", "Generates the hydrocarbons", "Measures the reservoir pressure"], 0, "An effective seal limits fluid movement out of the trapped reservoir."),
  lesson("Compare reservoir rock and fluids", "Reservoir quality depends on pore volume and connected pathways. Porosity describes how much void space is present; permeability describes how easily fluids flow through connected pores. Reservoir fluids range from oil and gas to water, and their properties influence phase behavior, recovery and facility design.", "Porosity = pore volume / bulk rock volume", "Two rocks with similar porosity can have different permeability if pore connections or pore-throat sizes differ. Core, logs, pressure data and production tests provide complementary evidence.", "Storage capacity and flow capacity are related but not interchangeable.", "Which property primarily describes a rock's ability to transmit fluid?", ["Permeability", "Porosity", "Bulk density"], 0, "Permeability describes connected flow pathways; porosity describes void-space fraction."),
  lesson("Classify resources and reserves", "The PEA series distinguishes conventional and unconventional resource styles and introduces resource and reserve classification. Resource estimates describe quantities with different levels of geological knowledge and project maturity; reserves are tied to discovered accumulations and defined development conditions. Classification is not a guarantee of production or profitability.", "Commercial reserve estimate = discovered quantity + defined project + commercial conditions", "A technically recoverable quantity may not be a reserve if there is no viable project, market, infrastructure or regulatory path under the classification framework being used.", "Always name the classification system, project basis and uncertainty range.", "What extra condition generally distinguishes reserves from a broad in-place resource estimate?", ["A defined, commercially supportable development project", "A larger seismic survey", "A single porosity measurement"], 0, "Reserve classification depends on a discovered accumulation and a defined commercial project, not only a volume estimate."),
  lesson("Follow the exploration workflow", "The posters cover basin screening, licensing, geological and geophysical interpretation, non-seismic methods, seismic acquisition, processing and interpretation, prospect definition, volume estimation and risk. Each stage reduces a different uncertainty; none turns an uncertain prospect into a certainty.", "Decision quality improves as independent evidence reduces uncertainty", "Acquisition records the seismic response; processing improves the data representation; interpretation converts patterns into a subsurface model. Check data quality and alternative explanations before ranking prospects.", "Separate observation, interpretation and decision throughout the workflow.", "Which stage turns recorded seismic measurements into an interpretable subsurface image?", ["Seismic processing", "Licence award", "Production allocation"], 0, "Processing corrects and organizes recorded data so interpreters can assess subsurface structure."),
  lesson("Estimate prospect volumes and risk", "Prospect evaluation combines reservoir area and thickness, porosity, hydrocarbon saturation, recovery assumptions and chance of success. Uncertainty in each input propagates into the recoverable-volume range. A risked estimate combines potential volume with the chance that the required geological elements work.", "Risked expectation ≈ unrisked recoverable volume × chance of success", "The chance of geological success is not the same as the chance of commercial development. Keep geological, technical, commercial and execution uncertainties visible rather than hiding them in one number.", "Use ranges and show the assumptions behind the estimate.", "What does applying geological chance of success do to an unrisked prospect estimate?", ["It weights the estimate by the chance the prospect works", "It proves the largest estimate will be recovered", "It removes uncertainty in reservoir quality"], 0, "Risking expresses expected value across uncertainty; it does not guarantee a particular outcome."),
  lesson("Connect licences, plans and project economics", "The series links petroleum rights and contracts with field-development planning and project economics. A development concept compares technical options, wells, facilities, schedule, market access, costs and fiscal terms. Investment decisions depend on the complete project and its risks, not only resource size.", "Project value depends on recoverable volumes, timing, price, cost, fiscal terms and risk", "A remote discovery may require pipelines or export facilities; those costs and schedule can change the preferred concept or make a smaller nearby opportunity more attractive.", "Economics follows the whole project from access to abandonment.", "Why might infrastructure access change a field-development decision?", ["It changes project cost, schedule and market access", "It changes the reservoir's original porosity", "It eliminates operating risk"], 0, "Infrastructure affects the economics and schedule of bringing production to market."),
  lesson("Evaluate formations and appraisal data", "Formation evaluation combines cuttings and mud logging, wireline or while-drilling logs, core and fluid samples. Well testing measures dynamic response under controlled conditions and can estimate deliverability and pressure communication. Appraisal wells and tests reduce uncertainty for a development decision.", "No single measurement describes the full reservoir", "A log may indicate a porous interval, while a representative fluid sample and pressure-transient test provide different evidence about saturation, fluid properties and connectivity. Reconcile the data rather than treating one tool as definitive.", "Use independent data types to test the reservoir model.", "What can a well test add beyond a static log response?", ["Evidence about dynamic flow and pressure behavior", "A direct measurement of every pore", "A guarantee of long-term production"], 0, "A test observes pressure and flow response over time and supports evaluation of deliverability and connectivity."),
  lesson("Plan a completion and stimulate deliberately", "A completion creates the flow path between the reservoir and surface and provides barriers and access for operating the well. Stimulation can improve communication with the reservoir; the PEA series highlights hydraulic fracturing and acidizing as distinct methods selected for different rock and damage mechanisms. Design and execution must be formation- and well-specific.", "Completion objective = establish controlled, maintainable communication with the reservoir", "Stimulation is not automatically beneficial: fluid compatibility, fracture containment, well integrity, produced-water handling and environmental controls all affect the design and outcome.", "Treat completion and stimulation as engineered, risk-assessed operations.", "What is the common objective of a well completion?", ["Provide a controlled flow path and maintain well barriers", "Create the reservoir itself", "Replace the need for production facilities"], 0, "Completion equipment provides controlled communication while preserving the well's required integrity."),
  lesson("Explain well flow and artificial lift", "Production depends on pressure energy and the resistance to flow through the reservoir, completion and surface system. Natural drive mechanisms change as reservoir pressure and fluid distribution evolve. Artificial-lift methods add energy or reduce the effective fluid-column load so a well can continue producing when natural energy is insufficient.", "Production rate is governed by reservoir inflow and system outflow", "A pressure change at the wellhead may reflect a surface constraint rather than reservoir decline. Diagnose the whole pressure path before selecting a lift change.", "Match the lift method to fluid, depth, rate, pressure and operating constraints.", "When is artificial lift generally considered?", ["When natural reservoir energy is insufficient for the desired production", "Before any reservoir is discovered", "Only when a pipeline is unavailable"], 0, "Artificial lift supports flow when natural energy cannot deliver the required rate."),
  lesson("Compare recovery and intervention choices", "The posters introduce primary, secondary and enhanced recovery, drive mechanisms, recovery factor, well intervention and decommissioning. Recovery methods target different limits and carry different facility, energy, cost and integrity requirements. Intervention restores or changes well function; decommissioning permanently closes and removes or leaves infrastructure under an approved plan.", "Incremental recovery must be weighed against cost, risk and lifecycle impacts", "A production problem may be addressed at the reservoir, completion, lift system or surface facility. Identify the bottleneck and evaluate intervention risk before choosing a workover or recovery project.", "Maximize responsible lifecycle value, not short-term rate alone.", "What is a primary purpose of well intervention?", ["Maintain, restore or change a well's operating capability", "Reprocess seismic data", "Set the original petroleum licence"], 0, "Intervention work accesses a producing well to diagnose, repair, modify or improve its operation."),
  lesson("Trace production through the market", "The last posters follow surface production facilities, offshore production systems, gathering and pipelines, tankers and storage, LNG, crude quality, refining, conversion, blending, petrochemicals and product distribution. Each transfer needs compatible specifications, capacity, measurement and quality control. The refinery product slate depends on crude properties and process configuration.", "Market-ready product = produced stream + processing + specification + reliable logistics", "Crude quality and price benchmarks are connected but not identical: composition, location, transport and contract terms affect the realized value. Refining separates and converts fractions; blending brings products within required specifications.", "Follow the material and its quality requirements through every handoff.", "What is the main purpose of product blending?", ["Meet a target product specification using compatible components", "Increase reservoir pressure", "Measure seismic travel time"], 0, "Blending combines streams to achieve required fuel or product properties."),
  lesson("Include safety, environment and the energy transition", "The final posters connect safety and regulation with emissions sources, carbon capture, utilization and storage, and hydrogen pathways. These technologies and controls have distinct lifecycle requirements, energy needs, infrastructure and risks. Their value should be assessed against measurable system boundaries, not labels alone.", "Lifecycle comparison requires a defined boundary, baseline and measured emissions", "A captured-carbon figure alone does not describe total climate impact; account for capture energy, transport, storage permanence and the product or process displaced. Apply current regulation and independent verification.", "Evaluate environmental claims with transparent boundaries and evidence.", "Which factors belong in a lifecycle assessment of a carbon-management project?", ["Capture, energy use, transport, storage and the displaced process", "Only the nameplate capture capacity", "Only the facility boundary fence"], 0, "A meaningful comparison includes the full defined lifecycle and what activity the project replaces."),
  lesson("Estimate operating cost and explain variance", "The Shell operating-cost chapter separates cost elements, time-dependent costs and periodic expenses, then connects estimates with cost reporting. A well-cost estimate is built from a defined scope, time basis, equipment, services and assumptions. Actual-versus-estimate variance should be explained, not just reported as a total.", "Well cost = time-dependent costs + services + materials + periodic expenses", "Separate rig time from one-off service or material costs. When actual cost changes, identify whether the driver was duration, scope, rates, logistics or an unplanned event before revising the forecast.", "A useful cost report preserves the assumptions and explains the variance.", "Which cost category usually grows with additional rig time?", ["Time-dependent costs", "A fixed one-time material purchase", "A historical benchmark with no time basis"], 0, "Daily rig and service costs generally accumulate as operating duration increases."),
];

const SUPPLEMENTAL_COURSES: LearningCourse[] = [
  {
    href: "/simulator",
    title: "Oil & Gas Value Chain",
    short: "Value Chain",
    description: "Follow a barrel from subsurface discovery through drilling, production, transport, refining and the products that reach customers.",
    icon: "layers",
    tags: ["Upstream", "Midstream", "Downstream", "Drilling lifecycle"],
    id: "value-chain",
    group: "Foundations",
    level: "Foundation",
    minutes: 32,
    outcomes: ["Distinguish upstream, midstream and downstream work", "Place drilling within the field lifecycle", "Explain how rig systems and well design connect to production"],
    sources: ["PEA, The Complete Oil and Gas Value Chain, posters 1-15: value chain, petroleum systems, basins, reservoirs, resource classification, exploration, prospect risk and petroleum rights (PDF pp. 3-17).", "PEA posters 16-25: rig systems, drilling, fluids and well control, casing and cementing, directional/offshore drilling, formation evaluation, appraisal, field development and completion (PDF pp. 18-27).", "PEA posters 26-40: stimulation, well flow, wellheads, recovery, lift, production facilities, intervention, midstream, pipelines, storage and LNG (PDF pp. 28-42).", "PEA posters 41-50: refining, products, petrochemicals, distribution, economics, safety/regulation, emissions, CCUS, hydrogen and the integrated barrel journey (PDF pp. 43-52)."],
    lessons: [
      lesson("Follow the barrel", "The value chain is commonly grouped into upstream, midstream and downstream. Upstream finds and produces hydrocarbons; midstream gathers, moves, stores and processes them; downstream refines, blends, distributes and sells products. Each stage depends on handoffs, capacity and product quality from the stages before it.", "Upstream → Midstream → Downstream", "A drilled well is an upstream asset. Its produced fluids may travel through gathering and pipeline systems, then reach processing or refining and distribution. The physical route varies by field and product.", "Every stage is connected, but each has different assets, economics and risks.", "Which stage primarily gathers, stores and transports produced hydrocarbons?", ["Upstream", "Midstream", "Downstream"], 1, "Midstream systems connect production to processing and markets through gathering, transport and storage."),
      lesson("Place drilling in the field lifecycle", "Exploration and appraisal reduce uncertainty about whether a resource can be developed. Drilling creates access to the reservoir; completion prepares a well to produce or inject; production operations manage the well and surface facilities over time. A discovery is not automatically a commercial project: technical, infrastructure, market and regulatory conditions also matter.", "Lifecycle: explore → appraise → develop → produce → retire", "A directional well may reach a target from an existing pad or offshore platform. That can improve access or avoid a surface constraint, while adding trajectory, torque, drag and well-placement considerations.", "A well is one part of an asset plan, not an isolated engineering calculation.", "What is the main purpose of appraisal after an exploration discovery?", ["Reduce uncertainty before committing to development", "Transport crude to a refinery", "Replace the production phase"], 0, "Appraisal gathers information to assess the resource and inform a development decision."),
      lesson("See the rig as five connected systems", "The PEA poster groups a rotary rig into power, hoisting, rotating, circulating and well-control systems. Power is generated and distributed; hoisting moves the string; rotating turns the bit; circulating moves drilling fluid; well-control equipment closes in and manages a well if formation fluids enter. The systems work together, so a change or failure in one can affect the operation as a whole.", "Rig function = power + hoisting + rotating + circulating + well control", "The mud pumps send fluid down the drillstring and through the bit. Returns rise through the annulus, carrying cuttings to surface; the solids-control equipment separates much of the cuttings from the returning fluid before recirculation.", "Trace the whole fluid path and identify which rig system acts at each point.", "Which rig system lifts and lowers the drillstring?", ["Rotating", "Hoisting", "Circulating"], 1, "The hoisting system uses the derrick, drawworks, blocks and line to handle the drillstring load."),
      lesson("Compare directional profiles", "A vertical well follows the simplest path when the target is directly below the surface location. Build-and-hold, S-shaped and horizontal profiles can reach offset targets, work around surface constraints or expose more reservoir interval. Directional reach has trade-offs, including higher torque and drag, cuttings transport challenges, steering complexity and additional survey work.", "Measured depth follows the well; true vertical depth measures vertical depth", "The PEA worked example emphasizes that at high inclination measured depth can increase much faster than true vertical depth. Pressure calculations use the vertical fluid column, so confusing MD and TVD can lead to serious errors.", "Choose a trajectory for the whole well objective, then check its operational costs and limits.", "Which depth represents the vertical height of the fluid column?", ["Measured depth", "True vertical depth", "Horizontal departure"], 1, "TVD is the vertical component and is used for hydrostatic pressure; MD follows the drilled path."),
      lesson("Understand offshore unit choices", "Water depth influences whether a unit rests on the seabed or floats. Jackups and platform rigs are bottom-supported; semisubmersibles and drillships float and use moorings or dynamic positioning. Floating units need a marine riser to connect the vessel to the subsea well-control stack, and vessel motion and emergency disconnect capability become important design considerations.", "Water depth and seabed conditions shape unit selection", "A marine riser is a conduit between the floating vessel and the subsea well; the subsea BOP stack is at the wellhead. They have different functions and are not interchangeable pressure barriers.", "Offshore well-control architecture depends on the unit, water depth and well design.", "On a floating drilling unit, where is the BOP stack located?", ["On the drill floor", "At the seabed wellhead", "Inside the marine riser tensioner"], 1, "For floating units, the BOP stack is installed at the subsea wellhead; the riser connects it to the vessel."),
      ...VALUE_CHAIN_EXTENSIONS,
    ],
  },
  {
    href: "/simulator",
    title: "HSE & Safe Operations",
    short: "HSE Foundations",
    description: "Learn to identify hazards, think in barriers, use site procedures, investigate incidents and understand the purpose of an operation-specific HSE case.",
    icon: "shield",
    tags: ["HEMP", "Bow-tie", "Barriers", "Incident learning", "HSE case"],
    id: "hse-foundations",
    group: "Foundations",
    level: "Foundation",
    minutes: 35,
    outcomes: ["Separate hazards, top events and consequences", "Distinguish preventive from mitigative barriers", "Explain how procedures, investigations and HSE cases support safe work"],
    sources: ["Shell WDLP HSE Management, v2.0.0: Topics 2-7 and 9 (PDF pp. 13, 19, 22, 30, 35, 41 and 48). This historical training source supports concepts only; follow current site procedures, company requirements and applicable regulations."],
    lessons: [
      lesson("Map the hazard and the event", "The Shell manual describes a Hazards and Effects Management Process (HEMP): identify hazards and potential effects, assess risk, then define controls and recovery preparedness. A bow-tie view places the top event in the middle, preventive barriers before it, and mitigative or recovery barriers after it. Escalation factors can weaken barriers and need their own controls.", "Hazard → preventive barriers → top event → recovery barriers → consequences", "For a loss-of-containment scenario, first identify the hazardous energy or material and the unwanted top event. Then ask which independent controls prevent the event and which reduce harm if prevention fails. Use the actual site risk assessment for real work.", "A hazard list is only the start; effective risk management also verifies barriers and recovery.", "In a bow-tie model, where do mitigative barriers sit?", ["Before the top event, preventing it", "After the top event, reducing consequences", "Outside the risk assessment"], 1, "Preventive controls act before the top event. Mitigative and recovery measures limit consequences after it."),
      lesson("Use procedures as working controls", "The Shell site-practices topic treats procedures and work practices as parts of a wider safety-management system. Their purpose is to make important controls repeatable, help people recognize abnormal conditions and plan for response. A procedure only works when it matches the task, is understood by the people doing the work and is supported by the right equipment and supervision.", "Effective control = suitable procedure + competent people + verified conditions", "Before a task, confirm the applicable procedure and the work scope, discuss hazards and barriers with the team, and pause if the conditions or equipment differ from the plan. Follow local induction, authorization and stop-work requirements.", "A document is not a barrier unless the work team can apply and verify it.", "What should a crew do when actual conditions no longer match the approved work plan?", ["Continue and record the difference later", "Pause, reassess hazards and follow site change/stop-work processes", "Assume the original controls still apply"], 1, "Changed conditions can invalidate assumptions; stop and reassess through the site's required process."),
      lesson("Investigate causes and barriers", "The incident-learning topic distinguishes immediate events from underlying causes and examines how barriers or controls failed. It recommends using investigations to capture learning and improve the management system. Potential severity matters: an event with a minor outcome may still have had a credible path to much more serious harm.", "Learning review = event sequence + failed barriers + contributing conditions + improvements", "Instead of ending an investigation at ‘the operator made an error’, ask what information, equipment, work design, supervision or procedure conditions shaped the action, and what barrier could prevent recurrence.", "Look for system conditions and prevent recurrence; do not stop at the last visible action.", "What is a stronger investigation question than simply ‘who made the mistake?’", ["Which barriers and conditions allowed the event to develop?", "How can the event be described in fewer words?", "Can the outcome be ignored if there was no injury?"], 0, "Examining barriers and underlying conditions produces more useful preventive actions than assigning blame alone."),
      lesson("Understand an HSE case", "The Shell manual describes an HSE case as a facility- or operation-specific demonstration of how the management system applies to the work. It should show that major hazards have been identified and assessed, controls and recovery measures are in place, and risk has been reduced to a level considered as low as reasonably practicable (ALARP). It is a structured argument supported by evidence, not a guarantee of zero risk.", "HSE case = safety argument + evidence + operation-specific controls", "A generic corporate policy cannot by itself demonstrate that a particular rig, well or activity is ready. The case needs a clear scope, credible hazard assessment, named controls, evidence that controls are implemented and arrangements for review when conditions change.", "An HSE case makes the safety argument reviewable and tied to a specific operation.", "What makes an HSE case operation-specific?", ["It connects assessed hazards and implemented controls to the actual facility or activity", "It repeats the same company policy for every operation", "It promises that incidents cannot happen"], 0, "The case should demonstrate how the management system and controls apply to the particular operation."),
      lesson("Make safety a personal practice", "The personal-responsibilities topic emphasizes that a management system does not make work safe on its own. People at every level influence safety through planning, decisions, communication and how they carry out work. The manual also connects good HSE management with learning, protection of health and the environment, and longer-term sustainable development.", "Safe work = system design + active participation + continual learning", "Use your role to understand the task, raise an unsafe condition, share learning from near misses and check that agreed improvements are actually completed. Use present-day company procedures and local law as the authority for action.", "Safety culture is visible in ordinary decisions, not only in policy documents.", "What happens to a safety-management system if people treat it only as paperwork?", ["It may have little effect on work as performed", "It automatically removes hazards", "It guarantees every barrier is effective"], 0, "The manual stresses that people have to apply and improve the system for it to influence real work."),
    ],
  },
];

const SHELL_DEEP_DIVES: LearningCourse[] = [
  {
    href: "/simulator",
    title: "Well Equipment & Rig Systems",
    short: "Rig Systems",
    description: "Connect rig selection, major equipment groups, hoisting, rotation, fluid systems and instrumentation to the work they support.",
    icon: "activity",
    tags: ["Rig systems", "Equipment", "Instrumentation", "Operations"],
    id: "well-equipment",
    group: "Shell Deep Dives",
    level: "Applied",
    minutes: 28,
    outcomes: ["Explain what the major rig systems do", "Trace the drilling-fluid circulation path", "Relate instrumentation to operational decisions"],
    sources: ["Shell Materials, Section 2: Capital Well Equipment, including Materials and Corrosion (PDF pp. 75-172), Wellheads (pp. 173-230) and Completion Equipment (pp. 231-344); Section 3: Sites, Rigs, Derricks and Masts (pp. 345-372), Rotary and Hoisting Equipment (pp. 373-442), Well Fluid Systems (pp. 443-510), and Rig Instrumentation (pp. 511-546).", "PEA, The Complete Oil and Gas Value Chain, posters 16-18 and 21 (PDF pp. 18-20 and 23)."],
    lessons: [
      lesson("Select equipment for the well objective", "A well-equipment program connects the well design and operating envelope to the rig, pressure-control equipment, tubulars, lifting systems and surface facilities. Selection must account for loads, pressure, environment, logistics, maintenance and the lifecycle stage, not only nominal capacity.", "Equipment fit = rated capacity + operating envelope + compatibility + maintainability", "Compare the planned well loads and pressures with the equipment limits, then confirm interfaces and contingency requirements. A nameplate rating does not prove the full system is suitable.", "Check the complete system and interfaces, not an isolated component rating.", "What should govern equipment selection for a planned well?", ["The well's loads, pressure envelope, interfaces and operating conditions", "The largest available nameplate value only", "The equipment's paint color"], 0, "Equipment must be suitable as a system for the conditions and loads it will encounter."),
      lesson("Trace power, hoisting and rotation", "The PEA rig poster groups core functions into power, hoisting, rotating, circulating and well-control systems. Power is generated and distributed; the hoisting system handles the string; the rotary or top-drive system transmits torque; circulating moves fluid; well-control equipment provides pressure-control capability.", "Rig function = power + hoisting + rotating + circulating + well control", "When drilling stops, the cause may sit outside the bit: a hoisting, power, rotating or circulation limitation can constrain the operation. Diagnose the function and its supporting equipment before changing drilling parameters.", "Map the function first; then trace its equipment and limits.", "Which function transfers rotary torque to the drillstring?", ["Hoisting", "Rotating", "Solids control"], 1, "The rotating system turns the drillstring and transmits torque to the bit."),
      lesson("Follow fluid through the rig", "The surface fluid system stores and conditions mud, pumps it down the drillstring and bit, returns it through the annulus, then removes or separates cuttings and recirculates the fluid. Tanks, pumps, standpipe, rotary hose, flowline and solids-control equipment each occupy a different point in this loop.", "Pump → drillstring → bit → annulus → flowline → solids control → active system", "A surface flow change can be caused by pump performance, line-up, returns measurement or downhole behavior. Check the whole loop and compare flow-in with flow-out before attributing a change to the formation.", "The surface system is part of the wellbore pressure and returns picture.", "Where do the returns go before the mud is recirculated?", ["Through solids-control equipment and active tanks", "Directly back down the drillpipe without inspection", "Into the subsea BOP"], 0, "Returns pass through surface processing and storage as part of the circulation loop."),
      lesson("Use instrumentation with context", "Rig instrumentation turns operating conditions into measurements such as hookload, rotary speed, torque, pump strokes, standpipe pressure, pit volume and flow. Sensors have range, calibration, response time and failure modes. A reading should be checked against related measurements and the current operating state.", "Measurement = sensor response + calibration + operating context", "When a pit-volume signal changes, confirm transfers, sensor status and flow-out behavior. A trend becomes more useful when aligned in time with pump and operational events.", "Instrument readings support decisions; they do not replace verification.", "Why compare a sensor trend with independent signals?", ["To identify lag, calibration issues or a genuine system change", "To make all readings identical", "To avoid recording the operating state"], 0, "Independent signals help distinguish process changes from sensor or context effects."),
      lesson("Read steel properties and corrosion risk", "Well equipment selection depends on material properties such as yield strength, tensile strength, stiffness and hardness, as well as manufacturing condition and environment. Corrosion, erosion, fatigue and stress concentration can reduce effective life. A material grade is not a substitute for checking service compatibility and inspection history.", "Design capacity depends on material + geometry + loading + environment", "A high-strength steel can still fail through corrosion, fatigue or an unsuitable connection. Confirm the applicable material specification, fluid environment, coating or corrosion-control assumptions and inspection criteria.", "Strength is one part of material suitability; degradation and service conditions matter too.", "Which combination best describes equipment material suitability?", ["Strength, geometry, loading and service environment", "Tensile strength alone", "The product name only"], 0, "Material performance depends on its properties, geometry, loading and environment."),
      lesson("Understand wellhead and completion equipment", "Wellheads support and seal casing strings and provide interfaces for pressure-control and completion equipment. Completion equipment provides the designed flow path and zonal access. The Shell equipment topics treat these as engineered assemblies with pressure, load, connection and installation requirements.", "Interface check = pressure rating + load path + connection + barrier function", "Confirm the equipment arrangement against the well schematic and the actual service conditions. A component's nominal rating does not verify its connection, seal or integration with adjacent equipment.", "Review the entire pressure-containing assembly and its interfaces.", "What should be checked when connecting two wellhead or completion components?", ["Ratings, connection compatibility, seals and intended barrier function", "Only their external dimensions", "Whether they were used on a different well"], 0, "Compatible ratings and interfaces are needed for the assembly to perform its intended function."),
    ],
  },
  {
    href: "/pore-pressure",
    title: "Geology & Petrophysics",
    short: "Geology & Logs",
    description: "Build a working subsurface picture from rock, structure, pressure, temperature, logs, cores, samples and dynamic well tests.",
    icon: "trend",
    tags: ["Geology", "Petrophysics", "Logs", "Formation evaluation", "Appraisal"],
    id: "geology-petrophysics",
    group: "Shell Deep Dives",
    level: "Applied",
    minutes: 30,
    outcomes: ["Distinguish geological description from petrophysical interpretation", "Combine logs, cores and samples", "Explain how pressure and tests reduce uncertainty"],
    sources: ["Shell Materials, Section 5: Geology for Well Engineers (PDF pp. 1133-1250) and Petrophysics (pp. 1251-1346); Well Control Principles and Practices, formation pressures and temperatures (pp. 645-798).", "PEA, posters 2-7 and 22-23: petroleum systems, reservoirs, fluids, formation evaluation, testing and appraisal (PDF pp. 4-9 and 24-25)."],
    lessons: [
      lesson("Build a geological framework", "Geology for well engineers connects rock types, sedimentary sequences, basin history, structure and the well plan. A subsurface model is an interpretation built from imperfect evidence; preserve the distinction between observed data and inferred geometry or properties.", "Subsurface model = observations + geological interpretation + uncertainty", "A mapped horizon may be tied confidently near a well and remain uncertain between wells. Track confidence and alternative interpretations when the model informs a target or casing decision.", "A model is a testable interpretation, not a direct picture of the subsurface.", "Which statement best distinguishes an observation from an interpretation?", ["A measured log response is observed; a lithology label inferred from it is interpreted", "Both are always direct measurements", "An interpretation requires no supporting data"], 0, "Measurements are observations; geological meaning is inferred using models and context."),
      lesson("Separate porosity from permeability", "Petrophysics estimates rock and fluid properties from logs, core, samples and other measurements. Porosity describes void space; permeability describes how connected paths transmit fluid. Saturation estimates how pore space is occupied, while net thickness and continuity affect how much reservoir contributes.", "Porosity = pore volume / bulk volume; permeability describes flow capacity", "A high-porosity interval may still deliver poorly if pores are poorly connected, fluid is immobile or the completion does not access the interval effectively.", "Storage, flow, fluid occupancy and continuity are separate questions.", "Which property primarily indicates connected-flow capacity?", ["Permeability", "Porosity", "Gross interval length"], 0, "Permeability reflects the ability of connected pore networks to transmit fluid."),
      lesson("Combine logs, cores and samples", "The PEA formation-evaluation poster and Shell petrophysics material point to complementary evidence: cuttings and mud logging, wireline or while-drilling logs, core measurements and fluid samples. Every method has a measurement scale, uncertainty, invasion or sampling bias and interpretation assumptions.", "Formation evaluation = multiple measurements reconciled at their own scales", "Use logs to understand continuous responses, core for direct rock measurements, samples for fluid properties and tests for dynamic behavior. Check depth matching and representativeness before combining them.", "No single log or sample tells the entire reservoir story.", "What is the main advantage of integrating independent data types?", ["They constrain different unknowns and expose inconsistencies", "They remove all measurement uncertainty", "They guarantee commercial production"], 0, "Independent observations provide complementary constraints and help test an interpretation."),
      lesson("Use pressure, temperature and tests", "Formation pressure and temperature influence fluid phase, density, well control, equipment and production. Pressure tests and well tests add dynamic evidence about reservoir connectivity and deliverability, while appraisal integrates these results into a development decision.", "Static logs describe local response; tests observe pressure and flow over time", "A well test can reveal pressure communication or changing deliverability that a static log cannot. Test design, operating conditions, gauges and interpretation assumptions all affect the result.", "Use dynamic measurements to test the static subsurface model.", "What does a pressure-transient or well test add to formation evaluation?", ["Evidence about dynamic pressure and flow behavior", "Direct knowledge of every pore's shape", "A guarantee of future commodity prices"], 0, "A test observes system response through time and supports inference about connectivity and deliverability."),
    ],
  },
  {
    href: "/cementing",
    title: "Completions & Production Operations",
    short: "Completions & Production",
    description: "Follow the well from completion architecture through wellhead equipment, production mechanisms, artificial lift, facilities and intervention.",
    icon: "cylinder",
    tags: ["Completion", "Wellhead", "Artificial lift", "Production", "Intervention"],
    id: "completions-production",
    group: "Shell Deep Dives",
    level: "Applied",
    minutes: 30,
    outcomes: ["Explain the completion's role in flow and barriers", "Relate reservoir drive to production method", "Identify production and intervention decisions across the lifecycle"],
    sources: ["Shell Materials, Section 5: Completion Operations (PDF pp. 799-928), Production Operations (pp. 1027-1066), and Well Control Equipment including BOP, choke and kill systems (pp. 1067-1132).", "PEA, posters 25-35: completion, stimulation, flow, wellheads, recovery, lift, production facilities, offshore systems and intervention (PDF pp. 27-37)."],
    lessons: [
      lesson("Design the completion around the objective", "A completion provides a controlled connection between the reservoir and surface while preserving required barriers and access. Open-hole and cased-hole approaches differ in how the reservoir interval is isolated and accessed. The design depends on the reservoir, well architecture, intervention strategy and operating envelope.", "Completion design = flow access + zonal control + integrity + intervention needs", "Evaluate how the completion will be installed, tested, operated and repaired. A design that produces initially but cannot isolate or safely access intervals may limit the field's lifecycle value.", "Plan for controlled flow and future intervention, not initial production alone.", "What is a central purpose of a well completion?", ["Provide controlled reservoir access while maintaining well integrity", "Replace the reservoir seal", "Determine the crude benchmark price"], 0, "The completion controls communication between formation and surface and supports well integrity."),
      lesson("Know the wellhead and tree", "The wellhead supports and seals casing strings at surface or subsea. A production tree provides valves and access for controlling flow and intervention. Well-control equipment, completion equipment and the tree have different functions and pressure ratings; their interfaces and barrier status must be understood for each operation.", "Barrier function depends on the component, configuration and verified status", "Before an operation, identify the barrier elements in the actual schematic, their tested status and which valves or plugs isolate each pressure source. Follow the site barrier standard; equipment diagrams are not operating instructions.", "Understand the installed barrier path, not only equipment names.", "What is a primary role of a production tree?", ["Control and provide access to the well's production flow path", "Support the rig derrick", "Separate cuttings from drilling mud"], 0, "A tree provides valves and access points for controlling the completed well."),
      lesson("Match production method to drive and flow", "Reservoir drive mechanisms provide pressure energy, while the completion and surface system impose resistance. As pressure and fluid distribution change, a well may need artificial lift. Lift methods add energy or reduce the effective fluid-column load; selection depends on depth, fluid properties, rate, pressure and available power.", "Production performance depends on reservoir inflow and system outflow", "A falling rate might result from reservoir pressure decline, water or gas changes, tubing restrictions, lift performance or surface backpressure. Diagnose the system before changing lift equipment.", "Treat a production decline as a system diagnosis, not a single-cause signal.", "What should guide artificial-lift selection?", ["Well depth, fluid, target rate, pressure and operating constraints", "Only the highest available motor rating", "The original rig type"], 0, "Lift selection is specific to the fluid, well, operating target and surface constraints."),
      lesson("Operate facilities and plan intervention", "Surface facilities separate and condition produced fluids before export or disposal; offshore systems add platform, subsea and flowline interfaces. Well intervention maintains, diagnoses or changes a well, while decommissioning permanently closes it under an approved plan. Each activity has integrity, environmental and production trade-offs.", "Lifecycle operations span well + facility + export route + abandonment plan", "A bottleneck can sit in the reservoir, completion, lift system, separator or export line. Locate it with pressure, rate, fluid and equipment evidence before committing to intervention.", "Follow the complete production path when diagnosing performance.", "Where can a production constraint occur?", ["Anywhere from reservoir inflow through the export system", "Only at the reservoir", "Only at the sales meter"], 0, "Production is a connected system, so constraints can exist at any stage in the flow path."),
      lesson("Understand well-control equipment as barriers", "The Shell well-control-equipment section covers pressure-containing equipment such as blowout preventers and associated control, choke and kill systems. Their purpose is to provide defined barrier and pressure-management functions within an approved well-control arrangement. Equipment configuration, ratings, testing and operation are governed by current standards and site procedures.", "Barrier readiness = correct equipment + verified configuration + tested function", "A schematic helps identify the intended flow path and barrier elements, but it does not replace the approved operating procedure or a competent well-control team. Verify the actual installed configuration and status before any operation.", "Know the system purpose and status; use the approved procedure for operation.", "What does testing a barrier element establish?", ["Evidence that it performed its defined function under the test conditions", "A guarantee against every possible failure", "The formation's exact pore pressure"], 0, "A test provides evidence of function within its stated conditions, not certainty for all future conditions."),
    ],
  },
  {
    href: "/drillstring",
    title: "Drillstring, Bits & Mechanics",
    short: "Drillstring & Bits",
    description: "Connect string components, loads, bit selection and formation interaction to drilling performance and operating limits.",
    icon: "link",
    tags: ["Drillstring", "BHA", "Bits", "Mechanics", "Bit performance"],
    id: "drillstring-bits",
    group: "Shell Deep Dives",
    level: "Applied",
    minutes: 28,
    outcomes: ["Describe load transfer through the string", "Explain bit selection trade-offs", "Use observed performance to guide diagnosis"],
    sources: ["Shell Materials, Section 6: The Drill String (PDF pp. 1347-1468) and Bits & Coreheads (pp. 1469-1612), including IADC roller-cone classification (Appendix 1, pp. 1547-1590) and PDC dull grading (Appendix 5, pp. 1591-1612); Section 5: Bits the Basics (pp. 929-1026).", "PEA, poster 17: The Rotary Drilling Process (PDF p. 19); poster 47: Oil and Gas Economics (p. 49)."],
    lessons: [
      lesson("Trace loads through the drillstring", "The drillstring transmits torque, axial load and circulation from surface to the bit. Drillpipe, heavy-weight pipe and drill collars contribute different combinations of tensile strength, stiffness and weight. Buoyancy, friction, dynamics, connections and well geometry influence loads along the assembly.", "Surface load ≠ local component load", "Hookload at surface is not the same as the force at the bit. Account for buoyed component weights, friction and operating conditions when estimating weight transfer or overpull margin.", "Follow the load path through every component and connection.", "What is a primary role of the drillstring?", ["Transmit mechanical load and drilling fluid to the bit", "Refine crude oil", "Measure only the surface pit volume"], 0, "The string transmits torque and axial load and provides a fluid conduit to the bit."),
      lesson("Understand the bottom-hole assembly", "The BHA places weight and stiffness near the bit and may include drill collars, stabilizers, motors, measurement tools and other components. Its configuration affects directional response, vibration, stress concentration and the location of compression and tension transitions.", "BHA response depends on component stiffness + geometry + operating load", "A stabilizer or motor can change trajectory behavior and mechanical loading. Compare the planned assembly with the actual tally and directional objective before interpreting a drilling response.", "The BHA is a designed system, not just weight below the drillpipe.", "Why does component placement in a BHA matter?", ["It changes stiffness, directional response and load distribution", "It changes the reservoir's original fluid type", "It eliminates survey uncertainty"], 0, "BHA geometry and stiffness affect how loads and directional forces act near the bit."),
      lesson("Select a bit for formation and objective", "Bit and corehead selection balances formation abrasiveness and strength, desired rate, durability, hydraulic cleaning, steerability and cost. Fixed-cutter and roller-cone designs use different cutting mechanisms. No bit type is universally best; offset performance and formation evaluation inform the choice.", "Bit choice = formation + well plan + hydraulics + run economics", "A bit that drills quickly but wears or fails early may increase total cost through trips and lost time. Compare footage, hours, dull condition, vibration and operating conditions together.", "Evaluate the whole run, not only peak rate of penetration.", "What is a sound basis for selecting a bit?", ["Formation, well objective, hydraulics, expected durability and total run cost", "Bit color alone", "The previous well's ROP without context"], 0, "Bit selection depends on the expected formation and operating conditions, with performance evidence."),
      lesson("Diagnose bit and drilling performance", "Observed ROP and mechanical response reflect bit-rock interaction, weight on bit, rotary speed, hydraulics, vibration, formation changes and bit wear. Compare intervals and actual drilling conditions before attributing a change to the bit. Dull grading and run records help improve future selections.", "Performance diagnosis = trend + operating context + formation + post-run evidence", "A sudden torque or ROP change may reflect a formation transition, cuttings loading, bit damage or control adjustment. Preserve the sequence of events and test likely explanations one at a time.", "Use run data to improve the next decision; avoid single-metric conclusions.", "Which evidence helps distinguish bit wear from a formation change?", ["Operating trends, formation context and the recovered bit condition", "Surface RPM alone", "The number of pages in the bit catalogue"], 0, "Multiple lines of evidence help separate tool condition from changing formation response."),
      lesson("Check pipe dimensions, grade and connections", "The drillstring chapter treats pipe size, weight, grade, connection geometry and load capacity as related design inputs. Tension, torque, internal and external pressure, wear and bending can govern different parts of the string. The limiting component or connection may be weaker than the pipe body.", "String capacity = component capacities + connection limits + combined load case", "Use the actual pipe tally and applicable manufacturer or standards data. Do not compare a single body rating with a combined operating load and assume the connection has the same capacity.", "The weakest relevant component and load case control the usable margin.", "What should govern a drillstring capacity check?", ["The weakest applicable component or connection under the relevant load case", "The strongest pipe-body rating", "The nominal string length alone"], 0, "A string is limited by the controlling component under the load conditions being assessed."),
      lesson("Use bit classification and dull grading", "The Shell appendices include IADC roller-cone classification and fixed-cutter bit dull grading. A consistent grading system records observed wear and damage after a run so performance can be compared across wells and bit designs. The grade is a description, not by itself a root-cause diagnosis.", "Dull record = observed condition + location/type + reason pulled + operating context", "Compare the dull record with drilling parameters, formation interval, run length and failure history before changing bit selection. Use the current classification standard and company reporting convention.", "Standardized condition records improve learning, but diagnosis still needs context.", "What is the main value of consistent bit dull grading?", ["Comparable records that support selection and performance review", "A guaranteed explanation of the failure cause", "A direct measurement of formation pressure"], 0, "A consistent grading language makes post-run condition comparisons more useful."),
    ],
  },
  {
    href: "/hydraulics",
    title: "Fluids, Hydraulics & Cement Systems",
    short: "Fluids & Hydraulics",
    description: "Bring drilling-fluid properties, pressure gradients, circulation hydraulics, well control, cement placement and casing barriers into one mental model.",
    icon: "waves",
    tags: ["Drilling fluids", "Pressure", "Hydraulics", "Cement", "Casing"],
    id: "fluid-systems",
    group: "Shell Deep Dives",
    level: "Advanced",
    minutes: 34,
    outcomes: ["Relate fluid properties to pressure and flow", "Trace losses and circulating pressure", "Connect casing and cement to well integrity"],
    sources: ["Shell Materials, Section 4: Fluids Technology (PDF pp. 547-600) and Cement Technology (pp. 601-644); Section 5: Well Control Principles and Practices (pp. 645-798); Section 7: Drilling Hydraulics (pp. 1613-1666) and Casing and Cementing, including casing preparation and running plans (pp. 1667-1748); Section 8: Drilling Fluids, including hole cleaning and hole problems (pp. 1749-1808).", "PEA, posters 18-19: Drilling Fluids and Well Control; Casing and Cementing (PDF pp. 20-21)."],
    lessons: [
      lesson("Treat drilling fluid as a designed system", "Drilling fluid transports cuttings, contributes to pressure control, cools and cleans the bit, supports the wellbore and interacts with permeable formations. Density is only one property; rheology, solids, chemistry, temperature and compatibility influence how the fluid behaves in the well and at surface.", "Fluid program = density + rheology + chemistry + solids control + operating limits", "A property measured at surface may change with temperature, pressure, contamination or solids loading. Track treatments and test methods when using fluid data to explain a downhole response.", "A mud weight value alone does not describe the fluid system.", "Which fluid function helps transport drilled cuttings to surface?", ["Suspension and circulation", "Wellhead isolation alone", "Crude distillation"], 0, "Circulating fluid carries cuttings through the annulus for separation at surface."),
      lesson("Connect gradients, pressures and the well window", "Hydrostatic pressure depends on fluid density and true vertical depth. The well must maintain adequate pressure against formation influx while avoiding fracture or loss limits. Temperature, fluid gradients, swab, surge and annular friction change the operating margin.", "Hydrostatic pressure (psi) ≈ 0.052 × MW (ppg) × TVD (ft)", "A measured-depth change does not equal the same TVD change in a deviated well. A static margin can also narrow during circulation if annular friction raises equivalent circulating density.", "Use correct depth and account for dynamic effects around the pressure window.", "Which depth belongs in the hydrostatic-pressure calculation?", ["True vertical depth", "Measured depth along the well", "Horizontal departure"], 0, "Hydrostatic pressure reflects the vertical column height, so TVD is used."),
      lesson("Budget pressure loss through the circulation loop", "Pump pressure is distributed across surface equipment, drillstring, bit nozzles and annulus. Flow rate, geometry and non-Newtonian rheology change the loss in each component. Hydraulics analysis connects pump limits, bit cleaning, annular transport, ECD and the fracture margin.", "Standpipe pressure ≈ surface + string + bit + annular pressure losses", "If measured standpipe pressure differs from a model, verify pump output, fluid rheology, nozzle configuration, flow path and operating state before changing the plan.", "Localize the pressure loss before responding to the total pressure.", "What can a component pressure-loss model help identify?", ["Where pump pressure is consumed through the circulation path", "The exact geology of every formation", "The sales value of produced crude"], 0, "A component model breaks total pressure into losses at distinct parts of the system."),
      lesson("Use casing and cement as a barrier system", "Casing supports the wellbore and isolates intervals; cement provides zonal isolation and supports the casing where designed. Pressure tests, centralization, displacement, fluid compatibility, returns and verification all affect the result. The cement job is a placement and barrier-quality problem, not only a volume calculation.", "Integrity = suitable casing + qualified connections + effective cement placement + verification", "Review both the planned cement volume and the evidence after placement, such as returns and test results. A theoretical annular volume does not prove that the annulus is fully cemented.", "Design the barrier and plan how to verify it.", "What does cement primarily contribute behind casing?", ["Zonal isolation and support", "The formation's original porosity", "A substitute for wellhead valves"], 0, "Cement supports the casing and isolates formations when correctly designed and placed."),
      lesson("Plan hole cleaning and diagnose hole problems", "Hole cleaning depends on annular velocity, fluid rheology, inclination, cuttings size and shape, pipe motion and time. Poor transport can lead to cuttings beds, increased drag, pack-off or difficulty making connections. The drilling-fluid chapter treats hole cleaning and hole problems as connected operational topics.", "Transport capacity depends on flow + fluid properties + geometry + cuttings", "When drag or torque changes, compare flow rate, rheology, inclination, cuttings loading and operational sequence before increasing flow or changing mud. Use the well program and approved limits for response.", "Hole cleaning is an evolving condition, not a single surface number.", "Which combination most directly affects annular cuttings transport?", ["Flow, rheology, inclination and cuttings properties", "Only surface mud weight", "Only the bit type"], 0, "Cuttings transport depends on the circulating fluid, well geometry and the particles being carried."),
      lesson("Connect pump output to hydraulic limits", "Pump hydraulics links displacement per stroke and pump rate to pressure losses through the surface system, drillstring, bit and annulus. Pump efficiency, liner configuration, fluid properties and operating condition affect delivered flow. A hydraulics calculation should respect equipment pressure and rate limits.", "Flow rate = verified pump output per stroke × strokes per unit time", "Check actual pump output and pressure response against the expected values. Higher flow can improve bit cleaning or cuttings transport but can also raise annular friction and ECD.", "A pump-rate change affects both cleaning and pressure margins.", "What is one important consequence of increasing circulation rate?", ["It can improve transport while increasing friction pressure and ECD", "It always reduces every pressure loss", "It changes measured depth"], 0, "Higher flow changes both transport and frictional pressure losses."),
      lesson("Plan casing running and cement placement", "The casing-and-cementing section covers planning, casing preparation, running equipment and cementing programs. Casing handling and centralization influence run loads and cement placement; fluid sequence, displacement volume, shoe track and verification are part of the job design. The actual program and barriers must follow current approvals.", "Job plan = casing tally + running conditions + centralization + fluid volumes + verification", "Track the casing tally, planned centralizer placement and volumes through the program. Compare actual returns, pump data and pressures with expectations and follow the approved response when they diverge.", "Execution data is essential to evaluate whether the planned barrier was achieved.", "Why does centralizer placement matter during cementing?", ["It helps position casing for the planned annular cement placement", "It changes the reservoir fluid type", "It replaces cement verification"], 0, "Centralization supports the intended casing position and cement distribution around it."),
    ],
  },
];

